      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Caribbean Dominoes",
          "subtitle": "Partnership Domino",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Caribbean dominoes: draw/block game, double-six. Four players in partnerships; play to empty hand or block. Regional rules vary (Jamaican, Haitian, etc)."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Domino games are enthusiastically played on many Caribbean islands. Typically the style of play is boisterous, and the pieces are slammed down on the table rather than placed gently. Most often there are four players in fixed partnerships. The rules vary from place to place; often the objective is for one team to win several rounds in a row (e.g. 4 or 6), which is characteristic of the Caribbean. Regional variants are documented for Jamaica (Partner and Cut Throat), Haiti, Puerto Rico (Chiva), and Cuba."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Setup",
          "subtitle": "Table, deck, equipment, and deal",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "4 in partnerships"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "28 tiles double-six"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Tiles"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Draw for hand; boneyard"
            }
          ]
        }
